WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
How can fertilizer spread far upstream leave the Bay's bottom short of oxygen?

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.900
The fertilizer never grabs oxygen itself.

3
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.540
Follow this cross-section from a field to the Bay's deep channel.

4
00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:18.700
The missing oxygen comes from a living chain, helped by summer water.

5
00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:26.450
A watershed is all the land that drains into one river, lake, or bay.

6
00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.470
Rain becomes runoff when it flows over the ground.

7
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:36.930
Orange nutrient markers join that water from fields, lawns, wastewater, and the air.

8
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.670
Here, nutrients mean nitrogen and phosphorus.

9
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.870
The orange markers reach sunlit surface water.

10
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:49.870
Green algae multiply, turning a thin layer into a thick bloom.

11
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.090
The bloom shades the gold grass below.

12
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.530
Some algae also grow on grass blades.

13
00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:01.150
Less light reaches the leaves, while dead algae begin sinking.

14
00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:05.313
At the bottom, bacteria break down the dead algae.

15
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:10.673
Bacteria are decomposers, living things that break down dead matter.

16
00:01:11.913 --> 00:01:14.373
They use blue dissolved oxygen while they work.

17
00:01:15.673 --> 00:01:18.973
Dissolved oxygen is oxygen gas mixed into water.

18
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:22.173
Fish, crabs, and oysters need it.

19
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:27.647
Now split the Bay into a cyan surface layer and a navy bottom layer.

20
00:01:28.787 --> 00:01:30.167
Both begin with oxygen.

21
00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:34.927
If the layers barely mix, which oxygen gauge will fall first?

22
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:42.680
The bottom gauge falls first.

23
00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:47.740
Fresh river water floats above saltier, heavier seawater in summer.

24
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.460
The boundary slows mixing.

25
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.540
Surface water still exchanges oxygen with the air.

26
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:60.000
Below, decomposition keeps using oxygen, but replacement is slow.

27
00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.740
Water this low in oxygen is hypoxia.

28
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:10.410
Warm water holds less dissolved oxygen, and bacteria decompose faster when it is

29
00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.790
warm. That strengthens the summer shortage.

30
00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:18.790
So the deep channel runs shortest on oxygen in summer, not winter.

31
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.760
The chain also has a delay.

32
00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:27.280
Much nitrogen arrives with high river flow in late winter and spring.

33
00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.680
Algae grow, die, and sink over time.

34
00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:37.260
Decomposition can continue into summer, so scientists use spring nutrient inputs

35
00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.660
when forecasting summer hypoxia.

36
00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:46.600
This process is eutrophication: nutrient enrichment feeds too much algae and can

37
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:47.880
lead to oxygen loss.

38
00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.980
Animals that can leave low oxygen move away.

39
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.560
Others may sicken or die.

40
00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.080
Follow the cause carefully.

41
00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:01.900
Nutrients start the chain, while living things use the oxygen.

42
00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:05.493
Low oxygen is not the only effect.

43
00:03:06.693 --> 00:03:09.973
The surface bloom and algae on grass blades block sunlight.

44
00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:16.733
That can weaken underwater grass habitat, a second harm that does not depend on low

45
00:03:16.733 --> 00:03:22.953
oxygen. Weather changes the outcome, but human nutrient inputs remain a pressure

46
00:03:22.953 --> 00:03:23.973
people can reduce.
